      "latency_ms": 2377.2,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 506,
      "output": "At its most fundamental level, **water** is a chemical compound and one of the most abundant and essential substances on Earth. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, looking at it through different lenses:\n\n### 1. The Chemical View\nChemically, water is represented by the formula **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**. This means every single molecule of water is made up of:\n*   **Two hydrogen atoms**\n*   **One oxygen atom**\nThese atoms are bonded together tightly. Water molecules are also \"polar,\" meaning the oxygen side has a slight negative charge and the hydrogen side has a slight positive charge. This polarity acts like tiny magnets, causing water molecules to stick to each other (which is why water forms drops and has high surface tension).\n\n### 2. The Physical View\nWater is unique because it is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three distinct physical states depending on the temperature:\n*   **Liquid:** What we commonly drink and what fills our oceans, rivers, and lakes.\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Formed when water freezes at or below 0°C (32°F). *Uniquely, solid water is less dense than liquid water, which is why ice floats.*\n*   **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Formed when water boils at 100°C (212°F), though it also evaporates into the air at lower temperatures.\n\n### 3. The Ecological and Biological View\nWater is the foundation of life as we know it. \n*   **For the Planet:** About 71% of the Earth's surface is covered by water, mostly in oceans (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water). It shapes continents, moderates global temperatures, and drives weather patterns through the water cycle (evaporation, condensation, precipitation).\n*   **For Living Organisms:** The human body is made up of about 60% water. It acts as a solvent, transporting nutrients, regulating body temperature, removing waste, and providing the moist environment necessary for cellular processes to occur. Without water, living things cannot survive for more than a few days.\n\n### Summary\nIn short, water is a simple molecule with extraordinary properties. It is the lifeblood of our planet, a driver of climate and weather, and the biological medium that makes life possible."
